69:- **RELIABLE / consistent**: one mode per run, carried to the peer at session open so the source walk and the destination's deletion scope read every pattern identically; `--ignore-case` applies to every pattern in the run — `--exclude`, `--include`, and every line of `--exclude-from`/`--include-from` — and never to the literal `--files-from` list (Q2, owner "a" 2026-11-20).
86:- [ ] **AC-8 (pattern files)**: `--exclude-from FILE` and `--include-from FILE` read one pattern per line (UTF-8; whitespace at both ends of a line and its line ending, LF or CR LF, are stripped — the rule `--files-from` already follows; blank lines and lines starting with `#` are ignored; a deliberate edge space is written `[ ]` — Q5, owner "a" 2026-11-20), each stripped line behaving exactly as the same pattern given on the command line, under the run's case mode; a line `*.jpg` followed by a space protects `a.jpg`, and a line `*.jpg[ ]` protects only the name that ends in a space; both repeatable and combinable with `--exclude`/`--include`; a missing or unreadable FILE refuses the run 
88:- [ ] **AC-11 (rsync rule lines, Q8)**: a pattern-file line that begins `- ` or `+ `, or holds only `!`, refuses the run before any transfer, prompt or note, naming the file, the line number and the reason (blit's pattern files hold bare patterns, not rsync rules); `[-] name` in a file names a real entry that begins dash-space. The `--files-from` list is not a pattern file and refuses nothing of the kind: a listed name beginning `- ` stays a name.
117:- rsync rule lines (Q8): after the shared reader has stripped a line, the PATTERN-file layer refuses one that begins `- ` or `+ ` or is `!` alone. The check sits above the shared reader, not in it, because the `--files-from` list must keep such a name as a name.
118:- One line reader for both list files (Q5): the line handling inside `FileFilter::load_files_from` — `str::trim`, which strips every Unicode whitespace character at both ends, then skip a blank line or one starting `#` — becomes a helper the pattern files share, so the two cannot drift apart.
119:- The saved job records the PATTERNS read, not the file path, so a replay does not depend on a file that may have changed (Q6, owner "a" 2026-11-20). This is how a `--files-from` list is already kept — by its lines as read when the job was made (`JobSpec.files_from`, `crates/blit-core/src/job_record.rs`), read once before the transfer starts (`capture_files_from`, review cr-jl5-1) — and how robocopy's saved job keeps its names (inline, measured 2026-11-20). The accepted cost: a pattern added to the file after saving is unknown to a replay, which will not protect what it names, until the job is saved again.
149:   - **Guards, each proven by mutation** (19 mutations, every one red, every file restored byte for byte): one row per way a pattern is read, chosen so only that reading can name the entry; `?` counting the same bytes in both modes, through the filter (`ſ.txt` named by `??.txt` in both and by `?.txt` in neither — the first design, folding the name, turns it red); includes follow the bit and the `--files-from` list never does; the deletion scope on a real directory; end to end through the binary on a local `mirror` (source-side skip, destination-only keep, unrelated extra deleted; as typed and with `--match-case` the same pattern names neither) and on `check` (AC-12).
177:3b. **Pattern files**: `--exclude-from` / `--include-from` in the CLI, read before validation; guards for AC-8 (file lines equal command-line patterns; comments and blanks; edge whitespace and CR LF stripped; `[ ]` keeps an edge space; missing file refuses; count wording) and AC-11 (`- `, `+ ` and `!` lines refuse with file and line; `[-] name` accepted; the `--files-from` list unaffected); the shared line reader lives in core beside `load_files_from`, no wire change.
179:   - **What is in it:** `--exclude-from FILE` and `--include-from FILE`, repeatable, on `copy`, `mirror` and `check`. The CLI reads each file into the command's pattern lists before anything else looks at them (`absorb_pattern_files`, called first in `run_command`; `check` reads them the same way), so validation, the filter, the wire, the deletion scope and the job record see patterns and never a file. The reader is core's (`FileFilter::load_pattern_file`) and shares one line rule with the `--files-from` list (`list_line`: both ends stripped, blank lines and `#` comments skipped); the rsync-rule refusal sits above that rule, so a `--files-from` name beginning `- ` stays a name.
181:   - **Beyond the plan's letter, the agent's call:** a UTF-8 byte-order mark at the start of a pattern file is not part of the first pattern (a file saved by a Windows editor would otherwise hold a first pattern that silently matches nothing — under a mirror, a deletion); and a NUL refuses the file, because a UTF-16 file of plain letters is valid UTF-8 with a NUL between each, again matching nothing. **`--files-from` does neither** and is unchanged, as AC-8 says: a list saved with a byte-order mark silently loses its first name. Older than this plan; for the owner (STATE, Open questions).
185:   - **Guards, each proven by mutation** (12 mutations, every one red, every file restored byte for byte): the reader line by line; the rule refusal with its line number and an advised spelling that is checked to name the entry; the two list readers agreeing on a line while only one refuses rules; the three kinds of unreadable file; the line's wording; and through the binary — file lines equal typed patterns, two files and typed patterns together, `--include-from` under the case mode, an edge space stripped and one written `[ ]` kept (Unix: Windows cannot hold a name ending in a space), an unreadable file refused before `mirror`'s question, rule lines refused on `mirror`, `copy` and `che
199:- **Slice 2's readings** are replaced where they disagreed with the matcher (cr-ecm3-5): a pattern is spoken of exactly when widening it for `--ignore-case` changes it — so `*[a-z]` and `*ß` are, which slice 2 passed over; a class is shown as the class of its letters' other case (`[A-Z]`), a letter in the case the option pairs it with (`STRAẞE`); a pattern whose reading changes in a way no spelling shows (a negated class) gets `… matches exactly as typed; capital letters matter (--ignore-case reads it otherwise).`; no line at all when `--files-from` overrides the patterns; and the line is one line whatever a pattern holds.
206:- **Q2's rule that the `--files-from` list overrides every pattern** did not hold at a peer for a list holding no name: empty and absent were one encoding on the wire, and the daemon applied the include. The spec now says a list was given (`FilterSpec.files_from_set`, field 10, inside contract 9 and spec version 4 — neither released) (cr-ecm4-2). Older than this plan.
217:- **Q2's rule that a list is the scope now holds for directories** (cr-ecm5-5): under `--files-from` a destination directory is a deletion candidate only when the list names it. Older than this plan, and true of every list, not only one that names nothing. With no list, a mirror still removes empty destination directories and no transfer carries one — a rule older than this plan, stated only in `check --help`, and put to the owner in `docs/STATE.md`.
225:- **The `--files-from` list is read once** (cr-ecm6-4), when the command begins, and every filter of the command is built from that content (`FilterInputs.files_from_read`) — cr-ecm5-3's known gap, closed.
231:- **The wire contract is 12 and the delegated request's spec version 7** (cr-ecm7-1): a `--files-from` name went to a peer as the text the sending machine writes a path in, so a Windows list's `sub\name.txt` was one file name to a Linux peer — older than this plan, but slice-3b-era deletion rules (cr-ecm5-5) lean on the list saying exactly what was listed. A name now travels as its components joined by `/` and is read piece by piece; a line that is no relative path of names (`./x`, `../x`, `/abs`) travels apart (`FilterSpec.files_from_unnamed`) and is reported as not found on every route, as before. Shown against real contract-11 builds, refused on push, pull and daemon to daemon in both
244:- **A source that is one file has one name** (cr-ecm9-3): the transfer's filter asked about the file's own name (since cr-ecm7-1), the walk `check` runs asked about the empty relative path, and the account of what a `--files-from` list asked for compared the list's name with the paths sent. AC-12 (`check` reads patterns as the transfer verbs do) did not hold for such a source. The walk asks about the root's base name and still emits the empty path; the account knows the one file by that name.
266:  - `513a7835` cr-ecm11-1 — a retry hands on the paths as they failed. The empty path in a list is the mark for the source ITSELF, which only a source that is a file answers to (`FileFilter::allows_root_file`); a directory put in the file's place holds no such entry, sends nothing and reports it missing. On the wire the mark is `FilterSpec.files_from_root`: **wire contract 20**. cr-ecm10-3's translation to the file's name is gone.
272:  - The delegated request: `files_from_root` and cr-uv2-1's directory mark went out under spec version 7 (cr-uv3-2, `utility-slices-r3`); closed by `19296bfb`, the bump to 8, which covers both.
281:  - `07bf16c1` cr-ecm12-1 — a retry's list says that it holds exact paths (`FilterSpec.files_from_exact`, **wire contract 22**, request version 10). A source that has become one file answers to the mark for itself and to no child's name; a `--files-from` list still names such a source by its name.
282:  - `bd84649f` cr-ecm12-2 — an incomplete copy a failed write leaves is stamped (its modification time, 1985-06-11T20:11:14Z), and the retry of an `--ignore-existing` run replaces a file at such a path only while it bears the stamp — it ran with the option off and replaced whatever stood there. Any other file is kept and the path reported as not copied (`FilterSpec.files_from_left`, **wire contract 23**, request version 11). The mark is one for every run; the record says what a recorded identity of the file would take instead.
288:  - `fc3df295` cr-ecm13-1 — the above; no time is stamped or read; `FilterSpec.files_from_left` now means "never written over"; **wire contract 25**, request version 12.
370:- ~~**Q2 — scope of `--ignore-case`**~~ — **settled 2026-11-20 (owner "a")**: every pattern in the run — `--exclude`, `--include`, and every line of `--exclude-from`/`--include-from` — and never the literal `--files-from` list, which stays exact names in both modes. `blit jobs retry` limits its session to exactly the failed paths through that list (`crates/blit-cli/src/transfers/retry.rs`), so it is unaffected. Rejected: folding the `--files-from` list too (one list line could select several files on a case-sensitive source).
371:- ~~**Q5 — pattern-file line handling**~~ — **settled 2026-11-20 (owner "a")**: option (a) below, after the owner asked for and received the §Prior art measurements and the `[ ]` measurement. The options as put (the recommendation changed that day once rsync's rule was measured deleting): (a) blit's own list-file rule — strip both ends and the line ending, skip blank and `#` lines, a real edge space written `[ ]` [recommended; it is the whitespace rule of robocopy's job file and of blit's own `--files-from`; its trailing half also matches git and GNU tar; rsync, git and GNU tar keep leading whitespace]; (b) rsync's rule — whitespace kept, `;` and `#` comments (measured: a trailing space 
378:  - **`check`** ("compare two trees … read-only verification") takes `--exclude`, `--include` and `--files-from` and builds its filter "via the same chokepoint that copy/mirror/move use, so `blit check --exclude '*.tmp'` matches `blit copy --exclude '*.tmp'`" (`crates/blit-cli/src/check.rs`). Left out, a `check` after a `mirror --ignore-case` cannot be given the same reading of the same patterns.
